    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. In my current case, the interruption isn't just a government mandate, but a series of unexpected, chaotic disruptions in the supply chain and local infrastructure that make it nearly impossible to maintain a consistent inventory. We're looking at whether these \"unforeseeable\" local disruptions fall under the umbrella of force majeure or if they're just considered a standard business risk. How do courts usually distinguish between a truly unforeseeable event versus one that is just disruptive or \"mischievous\" in its timing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Courts generally distinguish between true force majeure and mere business risk by examining whether the event was reasonably foreseeable at the time the lease was signed. If the supply chain issues stem from predictable, albeit heightened, risks in a regional market, a court might deem them a foreseeable operational hazard rather than an excusable event. For a disruption to qualify, the tenant usually needs to prove the event was exceptional, unforeseeable, and made performance objectively impossible, not just severely unprofitable or inconvenient.",
      "source": "target",
